[node name="Halfling Flavor Text" type="Label" parent="."]
visible = false
layout_mode = 0
offset_left = 487.0
offset_top = 155.0
offset_right = 1141.0
offset_bottom = 580.0
theme_override_colors/font_color = Color(0, 0, 0, 1)
theme_override_fonts/font = ExtResource("2_gdq3k")
text = "     Quick-witted and light on their feet, Halflings thrive in social settings. Due, perhaps, to their small 
stature, Halflings tend to have big personalities. Whether it be wise-cracks, a juggling act, or prowess 
with a flute and a lute, most Halflings have a reliable to way to break the ice and lighten the mood. 
Long before most histories can recall, Halflings and Humans found themselves benefiting from the 
other's company. Nowadays if a band of Humans doesn't have at least one Halfling in tow, it is an 
ill-fitted company indeed.
     Halflings are not just hangers-on however, they have a vibrant pastoral tradition and a seasonal 
festival at a Halfling village is sure to liven even the most melancholy of Elves. The vegetable gardens 
of such settlements are an art form in and of themselves. The stews, casseroles, and jarred goods created 
from their harvest could contend with many a royal's dinner spread. Famous too are Halfling 
leather-works and Halfling tanners may be the most accomplished the world over.
     Rarely called upon to field an army, Halflings nonetheless found a niche as scouts and ambush 
auxillaries. Such battlefield tactics apply to the individual as well. Dagger, dart, and well-oiled 
moccasins are the compliment of your average Halfling for hire.
     Ultimately Halflings are not an overly-ambitious lot. Halfling desires are understandable: shelter 
from the elements, safety from enemies, land to cultivate, and community to raise one's family within. 
With such quaint demands, Halflings are welcomed in all civilized kingdoms. Halfling Hamlets, most 
often located within partially wooded rolling hills, provide a peaceful and familiar respite no matter
 where in the world they may be found."

